/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 53 0 R
/E (Combining short interventions with extended discussion, the workshop aimed to map the field, strengthen connections, and identify directions for future research. Hosted at the UvA Institute for Advanced Study \(IAS\), the intimate and interdisciplinary setting encouraged sustained dialogue across disciplinary and geographical boundaries, creating the conditions for the collaborative exchange that the DATAGOV project seeks to foster.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
75 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 53 0 R
/E (Across two speaker sessions, a shared concern emerged around the shifting role of data infrastructures in governance: systems such as biometric identification, digital ID frameworks, education platforms, and social policy databases are increasingly shaping access to services, rights, and recognition. Rather than functioning as neutral tools, these infrastructures are becoming embedded in institutional processes and decision-making, raising questions about how governance is exercised when it is built into technical systems.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
76 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 53 0 R
/E (A recurring thread was the complexity of the actors involved. Participants pointed to the entanglement of public institutions, private companies, and third-sector organisations in the design and operation of data infrastructures. Cases from education and public administration illustrated how these systems complicate questions of accountability and control, challenging straightforward distinctions between public and private.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
77 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 53 0 R
/E (Empirical examples from Brazil, India, South Africa, and European contexts grounded these discussions in concrete settings. Cecilia Passanti and Mirca Madianou underscored how identification systems and biometric technologies are tied to longer historical trajectories, including colonial legacies, while also shaping contemporary forms of inclusion and exclusion. )
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
