80 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 54 0 R
/T (Contributions by Fernando Filgueiras and Bidisha Chaudhuri further highlighted how data infrastructures are transforming public administration and welfare systems, often in ways that reinforce existing inequalities. Speakers also reflected on how data-driven systems are reconfiguring governance practices. Discussions addressed the growing reliance on data and modelling in decision-making, the shifting role of expertise, and the limitations of existing frameworks such as transparency. Louise Amoore, Niels ten Oever, and Rocco Bellanova pointed to the need to critically examine the assumptions and logics embedded in these systems and their broader institutional effects.)
/E (Contributions by Fernando Filgueiras and Bidisha Chaudhuri further highlighted how data infrastructures are transforming public administration and welfare systems, often in ways that reinforce existing inequalities. Speakers also reflected on how data-driven systems are reconfiguring governance practices. Discussions addressed the growing reliance on data and modelling in decision-making, the shifting role of expertise, and the limitations of existing frameworks such as transparency. Louise Amoore, Niels ten Oever, and Rocco Bellanova pointed to the need to critically examine the assumptions and logics embedded in these systems and their broader institutional effects.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
81 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 54 0 R
/T (The final session moved into smaller group discussions on education, health, and biometric systems. These conversations returned to the tensions raised earlier in the day: the role of commercial actors in public sectors, the expansion of data collection and sharing, and the conditions under which individuals can access or opt out of these systems. Participants also reflected on methodological questions, including how to engage affected communities and study infrastructures that are often difficult to see or contest. The workshop established a common point of departure for the DATAGOV project and identified key areas for ongoing collaboration and research. Throughout the event, our live illustrator, MaryJet, visually captured and mapped the discussions, helping to synthesise and reflect the collective insights - which are also recounted in a DATAGOV Blog post.)
/E (The final session moved into smaller group discussions on education, health, and biometric systems. These conversations returned to the tensions raised earlier in the day: the role of commercial actors in public sectors, the expansion of data collection and sharing, and the conditions under which individuals can access or opt out of these systems. Participants also reflected on methodological questions, including how to engage affected communities and study infrastructures that are often difficult to see or contest. The workshop established a common point of departure for the DATAGOV project and identified key areas for ongoing collaboration and research. Throughout the event, our live illustrator, MaryJet, visually captured and mapped the discussions, helping to synthesise and reflect the collective insights - which are also recounted in a DATAGOV Blog post.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [1 122 0 R 3]
>>
endobj
