/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 56 0 R
/E (The second day of the DATAGOV Kick-Off moved from the workshop setting to Framer Framed, opening the conversation to a broader audience of researchers, students, artists, policymakers, civil society organisations, and members of the public. Through two keynote lectures and panel discussions, the event explored how emerging research questions around data infrastructures connect to pressing societal and democratic concerns, as we outlined in a dedicated Blog post. )
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [0 123 0 R 2]
>>
endobj
93 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Span
/K [1]
/Pg 14 0 R
/P 123 0 R
>>
endobj
94 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 56 0 R
/E (The morning keynote by Louise Amoore examined the relationship between AI, cloud infrastructures, and the changing nature of the state. Joined by Rocco Bellanova, Mirca Madianou and Niels ten Oever, the subsequent panel reflected on the infrastructural dimensions of AI, drawing connections between interoperability, humanitarian governance, and the growing dependence on digital infrastructures. Audience discussions extended these debates, raising questions about the future of public institutions and the implications of increasingly shared dependencies between states, private companies, and civil society actors.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
95 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 56 0 R
/E (The afternoon keynote by Fernando Filgueiras turned to Brazil's newly established National Education Data Infrastructure \(IND\), using it as a case study to examine how interoperable data systems are reshaping public administration. Framing digital transformation as a political rather than technological project, the keynote highlighted how infrastructures increasingly influence institutional coordination, governance, and state capacity.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
96 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 56 0 R
/E (The second panel, featuring Bidisha Chaudhuri, Cecilia Passanti and Rob van Kranenburg, broadened the discussion to digital public infrastructures across different national contexts. Speakers reflected on changing forms of statecraft in India, the historical development of digital identity and biometric systems, and Europe's dependence on global cloud infrastructure. Together, these perspectives reinforced many of the questions raised during the first day of the workshop, while highlighting their relevance across diverse political and geographical settings.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [5]
