/P 57 0 R
/T (Across the day, discussions repeatedly returned to questions of democracy, accountability and power. Participants considered how data infrastructures increasingly shape public institutions, redistribute authority, and challenge existing understandings of transparency, sovereignty, and public governance. Rather than offering definitive answers, the event underscored the need for continued interdisciplinary dialogue between researchers, policymakers, practitioners, and the public.)
/E (Across the day, discussions repeatedly returned to questions of democracy, accountability and power. Participants considered how data infrastructures increasingly shape public institutions, redistribute authority, and challenge existing understandings of transparency, sovereignty, and public governance. Rather than offering definitive answers, the event underscored the need for continued interdisciplinary dialogue between researchers, policymakers, practitioners, and the public.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
101 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 57 0 R
/T (The second day concluded the DATAGOV Kick-Off by expanding the conversation beyond the research community and reaffirming the project's commitment to examining how data infrastructures are transforming governance across different sectors and regions. Together, the two-day event established a strong foundation for future collaboration and identified a rich set of questions that will guide the next phase of the project.)
/E (The second day concluded the DATAGOV Kick-Off by expanding the conversation beyond the research community and reaffirming the project's commitment to examining how data infrastructures are transforming governance across different sectors and regions. Together, the two-day event established a strong foundation for future collaboration and identified a rich set of questions that will guide the next phase of the project.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
102 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 57 0 R
/T (Holding the public event at Framer Framed helped broaden the conversation beyond academia. As a space dedicated to critical dialogue between art, society, and politics, it provided an appropriate setting for engaging researchers, policymakers, artists, students, and members of the public in discussions about the societal implications of data infrastructures. The venue reinforced DATAGOV's commitment to connecting academic research with wider public debate.)
/E (Holding the public event at Framer Framed helped broaden the conversation beyond academia. As a space dedicated to critical dialogue between art, society, and politics, it provided an appropriate setting for engaging researchers, policymakers, artists, students, and members of the public in discussions about the societal implications of data infrastructures. The venue reinforced DATAGOV's commitment to connecting academic research with wider public debate.)
/Pg 15 0 R
