/Title (Contributions by Fernando Filgueiras and Bidisha Chaudhuri further highlighted how data infrastructures are transforming public administration and welfare systems, often in ways that reinforce existing inequalities. Speakers also reflected on how data-driven systems are reconfiguring governance practices. Discussions addressed the growing reliance on data and modelling in decision-making, the shifting role of expertise, and the limitations of existing frameworks such as transparency. Louise Amoore, Niels ten Oever, and Rocco Bellanova pointed to the need to critically examine the assumptions and logics embedded in these systems and their broader institutional effects.)
/Dest [12 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 229 0 R
/Next 293 0 R
>>
endobj
264 0 obj
<<
/Title (Holding the public event at Framer Framed helped broaden the conversation beyond academia. As a space dedicated to critical dialogue between art, society, and politics, it provided an appropriate setting for engaging researchers, policymakers, artists, students, and members of the public in discussions about the societal implications of data infrastructures. The venue reinforced DATAGOV's commitment to connecting academic research with wider public debate.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 294 0 R
/Next 230 0 R
>>
endobj
