293 0 obj
<<
/Title (The final session moved into smaller group discussions on education, health, and biometric systems. These conversations returned to the tensions raised earlier in the day: the role of commercial actors in public sectors, the expansion of data collection and sharing, and the conditions under which individuals can access or opt out of these systems. Participants also reflected on methodological questions, including how to engage affected communities and study infrastructures that are often difficult to see or contest. The workshop established a common point of departure for the DATAGOV project and identified key areas for ongoing collaboration and research. Throughout the event, our live illustrator, MaryJet, visually captured and mapped the discussions, helping to synthesise and reflect the collective insights - which are also recounted in a DATAGOV Blog post.)
/Dest [12 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 263 0 R
/Next 323 0 R
>>
endobj
294 0 obj
<<
/Title (The second day concluded the DATAGOV Kick-Off by expanding the conversation beyond the research community and reaffirming the project's commitment to examining how data infrastructures are transforming governance across different sectors and regions. Together, the two-day event established a strong foundation for future collaboration and identified a rich set of questions that will guide the next phase of the project.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 324 0 R
/Next 264 0 R
>>
endobj
